      "question": {
        ":question/id": "64a71b0b-c6d6-5cc3-89f8-a2979683d0ff",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{(x-5)}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-(x-5)y.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x-5>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which all displayed real expressions are defined. Take $y>0$.\n\n{{selection}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-5)}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-(x-5)y \\\\ {(x-5)}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x-5)y & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{(x-5)}^{-1}y & ={(x-5)}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={(x-5)}^{-1},\\,q(x)={(x-5)}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{(x-5)}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={(x-5)}^{-2}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\u2217)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{(x-5)}^{-1}z={(x-5)}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (x-5)},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{(x-5)}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (x-5)})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-5)} \\\\  & ={(x-5)}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={(x-5)}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-5)}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-1}z) & ={(x-5)}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-2}) \\\\ {(x-5)}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({(x-5)}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({(x-5)}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{(x-5)}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {(x-5)}^{3/2}z & =3{(x-5)}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{(x-5)}+C}{{(x-5)}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{(x-5)}+C}{{(x-5)}^{3/2}}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{(x-5)}+C)}^{2/3}}{(x-5)}\\,.\n$$\n\nThese are the nonzero real solutions on the specified intervals; exclude denominator zeros and restrict constants and intervals to the real branches used above. Since $z=y^{3/2}>0$, retain only intervals where the displayed expression for $z$ is positive.",
